Federated learning lets many phones or devices train one shared model without sending their data to a server. Each device trains locally and only sends back an update.

That privacy has a cost. The server never sees the data, so it cannot check whether the labels are honest.

## The usual attack

The classic attack is label flipping. A bad client changes some of its labels at random. A cat becomes a truck. A shirt becomes a sneaker.

It hurts the model, but it is loud. Random flips push the update in strange directions, and many defences are built to spot exactly that.

## A quieter version

My co-authors and I asked a different question. What if the attacker flipped labels toward classes that already look alike?

A cat labelled as a dog is believable. A cat labelled as a truck is not.

Our method, FBPLF, measures how close each class is in feature space and flips each label toward its nearest neighbours. We tested it on CIFAR-10 and Fashion-MNIST with 50 clients over 100 rounds.

## What happened

At the heaviest poisoning level on CIFAR-10, random flipping crushed the model to **2.15 percent** accuracy. FBPLF left it at **14.9 percent**.

That sounds like the random attack is stronger. It is, if your only goal is to break everything. But broken models get noticed.

FBPLF does something more dangerous. It pushes the errors onto one target. Cats turn into dogs. T-shirts turn into shirts. And a cosine-similarity detector flagged it less often than random flipping, because its updates stay close to honest ones.

> The most dangerous attack is not the loudest one. It is the one that looks like a normal mistake.

This paper is under review at IEEE ICASSP 2027. The code is on my GitHub.
